<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Intelligent Robots</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Technology Changes Society</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program. Interactive: Facilitate discussions on future career paths.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret and explain instructions on using a professional networking site (e.g., "Some steps include choosing a platform, creating an account, and entering your experience, such as internships in tech hubs in Panama like Ciudad del Saber.")<br>Can facilitate and lead a group discussion on career interests, guiding classmates to share their goals (e.g., "What skills do you think are important for a career in technology?")<br>Can interpret classmates' career-related feedback during discussions on job skills (e.g., "How would you improve your teamwork skills?")</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates students' prior knowledge, introduces the topic, and pre-teaches key vocabulary and pronunciation for effective listening comprehension.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of different types of robots (industrial, domestic, humanoid) and asks students to listen to short, descriptive sentences about each image. Students raise their hands when they hear a word related to "technology" or "robots." For example, the teacher says: "This *robot* is used in factories to *advance* production" or "Many people believe *artificial intelligence* will *revolutionize* our *society*." This sparks curiosity and connects to the theme "How Technology Changes Society."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary orally, writing each word on the board after pronunciation. Focus words: "artificial intelligence," "robots," "technology," "society," "opportunities," "challenges," "job markets," "evolve," "revolutionize," "innovative." The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of each word, specifically emphasizing the long /ɑː/ sound in "robot," "job," and "start" (if applicable) and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like "innovative" /ˈɪn.ə.veɪ.tɪv/ and "technological" /ˌtek.nəˈlɒdʒ.ɪ.kəl/. Students repeat chorally and individually. The teacher also models sentences using future simple and conditional forms, e.g., "If AI continues to evolve, it might change the job market significantly."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'artificial intelligence' something natural or created by humans?" (Expected answer: Created by humans.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a robot 'evolves,' does it stay the same or change over time?" (Expected answer: It changes over time, it develops.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'revolutionize' mean making small changes or big, significant changes?" (Expected answer: Big, significant changes.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are exposed to new language in an authentic context through listening, focusing on gist and specific details related to the "Intelligent Robots" scenario.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Intelligent Robots":</b> Students will listen to a short audio clip (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) from a podcast segment discussing the impact of AI and robots on future "job markets" and "careers." The audio will include instructions or descriptions of how new "technology" is "revolutionizing" various sectors, mentioning "opportunities" and "challenges." It will contain examples of conditional forms, future simple, and passive voice. For example: "AI systems are programmed to learn from data and perform tasks."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio podcast segment about the "future of work" and "AI" (authentic register for general audience). Genre: Informative discussion. Format: Audio recording.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (first listening):</b> Students listen once and answer: "What is the main topic of this podcast segment?" and "Does the speaker seem optimistic or concerned about the future of technology?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (second listening):</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True or False based on specific details mentioned in the audio. E.g., "AI will only create challenges, not opportunities." (False) "Some jobs will be automated by intelligent robots." (True)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (third listening):</b> Students match key vocabulary words heard in the audio (e.g., "algorithms," "data," "software," "functionality," "systems") with their definitions or examples provided on a handout.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled listening activities to reinforce understanding and accurate recognition of target language and pronunciation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice interpreting specific instructions and identifying key vocabulary (e.g., "programming languages," "guidelines," "updates," "improvements," "customer service") and grammatical structures (conditional forms, future simple, passive voice) from spoken input. Focus on distinguishing pronunciation of target vocabulary.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/Cloze Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio segment again, but this time, students have a transcript with specific target vocabulary words (e.g., "algorithms," "data," "employment," "systems") blanked out. Students listen carefully and fill in the missing words. This reinforces listening for specific information and vocabulary recognition. E.g., "If *AI* continues to evolve, it might change the *job market* significantly."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Pronunciation Discrimination:</b> The teacher says pairs of words, some with the /ɑː/ sound and some with similar but different vowel sounds (e.g., "robot" vs. "robber," "job" vs. "J.O.B." (spelling out)). Students listen and identify if the target sound /ɑː/ is present. Then, the teacher says multisyllabic words like "innovative," "technological," "artificial," "significant." Students listen and mark the stressed syllables.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Listening for Instructions):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays a short audio clip (e.g., "Voice instructions for setting up a basic professional networking profile" or "How to update a specific software functionality"). One student listens and notes down 3-4 key instructions using target verbs like "establish," "create," "enter," "control," "update." The other student listens and checks if their partner’s notes are accurate and complete. They discuss any discrepancies, using phrases like "If you missed that step, it means..." or "The instruction was to 'enhance' the profile, not 'establish' it yet."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the acquired language and listening skills in a communicative task, applying them to the scenario of "Intelligent Robots" and the theme "How Technology Changes Society."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</b> Students will participate in a guided group discussion on "Future Careers in an AI-Driven World."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion (3-4 students per group).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher assigns each group a role (e.g., "AI ethicist," "Tech entrepreneur," "Future job seeker," "Sociologist").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a short audio prompt (1-2 sentences) about a specific aspect of AI's impact on careers (e.g., "Listen to this news report: 'A new study predicts that *intelligent robots* will take over many *customer service* jobs in the next decade.'").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to their prompt and then, within their groups, lead and facilitate a discussion on related career interests and necessary skills. They must actively listen to their classmates' contributions and integrate them.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use conditional forms (e.g., "If *AI continues to advance*, new *opportunities* will emerge."), future simple (e.g., "AI *will create* new *employment* options."), and passive voice (e.g., "New skills *will be required*.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They practice pragmatic functions like "Asking about AI's future impact" (e.g., "How will *AI change* the *job market*?") and sociolinguistic functions like "Discussing AI's societal role sensitively" (e.g., "AI brings new possibilities but also some *challenges* for the future.").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage evaluates students' listening comprehension, their ability to interpret instructions, and their capacity to interact using the target language.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on listening comprehension during group discussion and active participation. Peer assessment will also be used.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will actively participate in the group discussion, demonstrating their ability to:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpret the initial audio prompt and integrate its information into the discussion.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listen to and interpret classmates' career-related feedback and goals.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitate and lead portions of the discussion, guiding others to share.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use target vocabulary and grammar (conditional, future simple, passive voice) heard in context.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Interpretation:</b> Accurately interprets the main idea and specific details from audio prompts and peer input.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Active Participation & Facilitation:</b> Actively listens, asks relevant questions, and encourages group members to contribute (e.g., "What skills do you think are important for a career in technology?").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Appropriately uses target vocabulary (e.g., "artificial intelligence," "job markets," "opportunities," "challenges," "evolve") and grammar (conditionals, future simple, passive voice) in context.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Pronunciation:</b> Speaks clearly, with intelligible pronunciation of key sounds like /ɑː/ and appropriate stress in multisyllabic words.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing you learned today about how technology changes society, based on what you heard?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did you listen to your classmates' ideas during the discussion?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages learners and the teacher to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific challenges did you face when listening to the instructions or podcast, and how might you improve your listening skills next time?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How comfortable do you feel now interpreting common instructions related to technology or career discussions?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, particularly concerning the listening activities and the introduction of vocabulary and pronunciation? (e.g., "The podcast segment engaged students well, and the pronunciation focus on /ɑː/ helped clarify key terms.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension)? (e.g., "Some students struggled with faster speech in the podcast; I will provide a slightly slower audio for the reading comprehension activity's pre-listening task in Lesson 2. I also need to ensure the target vocabulary from today is explicitly present in the reading text for the next lesson.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced key vocabulary and concepts about "Intelligent Robots" and "How Technology Changes Society" through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build on this foundation by presenting a written text using the same core vocabulary and themes, allowing students to reinforce their understanding in a new modality.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>